/*  debugAllT -- `debug ALL <rule>` MARKS A RULE'S WHOLE SUBTREE, AND THE MARK IS PER-WALK.

    `debug X` marks the rule X. `debug ALL X` marks X and every component below it, turtles all
    the way down, via GroupItem::setDebug(). `ALL` is TEXT, not grammar -- aCTionDEBUG matches
    it by string exactly as it matches GUARD, and the grammar has never heard of either.

    ⚠ THE ROWS ASSERT THE COUNT, NOT THE FLAGS, AND THAT IS ON PURPOSE. There is no kant
    accessor for `debugged` -- it is a GroupBody flag with no GroupFields entry -- so a fixture
    cannot read the marks directly. setDebug returns how many nodes it marked, aCTionDEBUG
    prints that number unconditionally, and the number only moves when the walk moves. It is a
    better instrument than the flags would be: a flag read says one node was marked, the count
    says the whole walk happened.

    ⚠⚠ DB-4 AND DB-5 ARE THE ONLY ROWS THAT SEE THE PER-WALK PROPERTY, AND THEY EXIST BECAUSE
    THE FIRST CUT OF setDebug DID NOT HAVE IT. Using `debugged` itself as the visited mark is
    idempotent within one walk and WRONG ACROSS TWO: the mark is per-process, so a component
    marked by an earlier command stops the next walk dead at that node and leaves everything
    below it unmarked. That is parseWalked's own over-refusal arriving at a new seat. The cure
    is the clear pass in setDebug -- clearDebug() runs first and blanks the reachable set, so
    every walk starts from the same place. DB-4 drives it with an OUTSIDE mark and DB-5 with the
    walk's own leftovers; without the clear pass DB-4 truncates and DB-5 reads 0.

    ⚠ DB-1..DB-3 ARE A LADDER, NOT THREE SAMPLES. dbgLeaf is flat, dbgMid holds a reference to
    dbgLeaf, dbgTop holds a reference to dbgMid -- so the counts must INCREASE, and a walk that
    stopped at depth 1 would read the same number for all three. Depth is what is asserted; the
    absolute numbers are whatever the structure gives and they are pinned as measured.

    ⚠ DB-6 IS THE REFUSAL. `debug ALL;` naming no rules is a spelling that parses and would
    otherwise do nothing, which is the family this project has paid for repeatedly. It refuses
    by name instead. Its anti-vacuity sibling is every row above it: they print counts and no
    refusal, so DB-6 cannot pass by the refusal machinery being broken in the always-on
    direction.  */
Start();
register(Grokking);
define
    dbgLeaf     "leafA" "leafB";
    dbgMid      dbgLeaf "midX";
    dbgTop      dbgMid "topY";
    ;
search Grokking list;

cerr "=== DB-1 flat rule ===":;
debug ALL dbgLeaf;

cerr "=== DB-2 one level down ===":;
debug ALL dbgMid;

cerr "=== DB-3 two levels down ===":;
debug ALL dbgTop;

cerr "=== DB-4 an OUTSIDE mark must not truncate the walk ===":;
debug dbgLeaf;
debug ALL dbgTop;

cerr "=== DB-5 the walk's own leftovers must not truncate it either ===":;
debug ALL dbgTop;

cerr "=== DB-6 ALL naming no rules refuses by name ===":;
debug ALL;

print "DEBUGALLT SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();
